      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Context",
          "content": "Longitudinal data of children with Prader-Willi syndrome (PWS) treated with genotropin were registered in the Pfizer International Growth Database (KIGS)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Objective",
          "content": "To evaluate efficacy and safety of growth hormone (GH) treatment in a large group of children with PWS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Design",
          "content": "Data registered in KIGS from 1987 to 2012."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Setting",
          "content": "Worldwide retrospective cohort study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Patients",
          "content": "Patients included 522 prepubertal children treated with GH for three years and 173 children who had reached adult height. Safety analysis included 2332 children. Intervention involved GH treatment."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Main outcome measure",
          "content": "Height standard deviation score (SDS), body mass index (BMI) SDS, occurrence of serious adverse events, and deaths reported in KIGS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Results",
          "content": "In prepubertal children, mean (standard deviation) height SDS improved to -0.31 (1.34) (P < 0.05) during three years of GH treatment. In the adolescent group, height SDS improved until the start of puberty to -0.22 (1.31) (P < 0.05) but had a loss of -0.77 (0.81) during puberty, resulting in a mean adult height SDS of -1.19 (1.37). Total height gain was 0.95 (1.32) SDS. BMI SDS increased in the prepubertal group from 1.11 (2.09) to 1.53 (1.43) (P < 0.05) and did not significantly change in the adolescent group, who had a BMI SDS at an adult height of 1.78 (1.26). KIGS contained 12 death reports."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Conclusions",
          "content": "GH treatment in children with PWS significantly improves linear growth. BMI remains on average below +2 SDS, in contrast to the natural course of increasing obesity in PWS. Safety should be closely monitored in children with PWS, with and without GH treatment."
        }
      ]},
